!
! TCPIP$SERVICE.DAT - TCP/IP Services for OVMX auxiliary-server (TCPIP$INETD)
! service database (rd vms-cdb9).
!
! OVMX-DEFINED FORMAT (CLAUDE.md Rule 8): only the auxiliary-server BEHAVIOUR
! (a master listener that binds each enabled service's well-known port and
! spawns the service's image on an inbound connection) is observed from the
! public VSI OpenVMS TCP/IP Services documentation. VSI does NOT publish the
! byte-level layout of its internal service store, so THIS LINE FORMAT is an
! OVMX invention -- the same whitespace-separated text idiom as
! SYS$STARTUP:VMS$VMS.DAT -- parsed by tcpip_inetd_parse_db()
! (src/vmstcpip/services/tcpip_inetd.h). It is NOT presented as VMS-authentic.
!
! One ENABLED service per line:
!
!     service-name  port  [run-as-user]  image-filespec  [image-args...]
!
! separated by whitespace. "port" is the decimal TCP port the auxiliary server
! binds over the executive BGn: seam. "run-as-user" is OPTIONAL: a SYSUAF
! username the auxiliary server drops to (its UIC + authorized privileges) via
! vms_kif_setident before executing the service image (rd vms-8bd, R4 G1) -- so
! a service runs under its own low-privilege account, never SYSTEM. It is
! identified positionally as the token that carries NO device colon and NO
! slash; because "image-filespec" always carries a device colon
! (e.g. SYS$SYSTEM:...), the two never collide. A line with no run-as-user token
! FAILS CLOSED: the auxiliary server refuses to launch that service rather than
! run it privileged (fail-closed is the security fix, not a SYSTEM fallback).
! "image-filespec" is the service image the auxiliary server runs on an inbound
! connection (with the accepted connection as its SYS$INPUT/SYS$OUTPUT); any
! trailing tokens are passed to the image as arguments. Lines beginning with
! "!" and blank lines are comments/ignored.
!
! To enable a service, add its line here; the auxiliary server binds it when
! @SYS$STARTUP:TCPIP$STARTUP starts TCPIP$INETD.EXE.
!
! SSH is delivered by VMSSSHD.EXE, launched by the auxiliary server (rd vms-9ef).
! It is listed here but commented out until VMSSSHD.EXE ships in the bootable
! image; uncomment to enable once present (do NOT fake an unbound service).
!
! SSH   22  TCPIP$SSH  SYS$SYSTEM:VMSSSHD.EXE
!
! DAYTIME (RFC 867), rd vms-477 -- the first ENABLED service: a genuine
! standalone image (TCPIP$DAYTIME.EXE) the auxiliary server launches end to end
! over the executive. On an inbound connection to port 13 the auxiliary server
! drops to the TCPIP$DAYTIME service account and spawns TCPIP$DAYTIME.EXE with
! the accepted connection as its SYS$INPUT/SYS$OUTPUT; the image writes the
! current date/time as a one-line ASCII string (RFC 867) and exits.
! DAYTIME 13 TCPIP$DAYTIME SYS$SYSTEM:TCPIP$DAYTIME.EXE
!   ^ DISABLED by default (rd vms-21b). The shipped default auto-starts NO network
!     service: this line is commented AND SYSTARTUP_VMS.COM does not invoke
!     @SYS$STARTUP:TCPIP$STARTUP, so TCPIP$INETD never runs and nothing binds a
!     port out of the box. (It never actually served before either -- INETD was
!     never staged/started -- so this is honest, not a regression.) Enabling a
!     service by default = an operator security-posture call. The daytime
!     cold-boot proof enables it via a TEST overlay
!     (distro/rootfs-test-tcpip/, --build-arg OVMX_TEST_ENABLE_TCPIP=1), never
!     the shipped image. tests/integration/test_tcpip_posture_guard.sh enforces
!     this (reds if a future change ships an enabled service or wires TCPIP$STARTUP).
